# Line endings, pinned so that a clone on Windows cannot turn two of this repo's gates into
# line-ending reports.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which writes CRLF into the working tree while the
# repository stores LF. With nothing pinned here, that cost this project a session: `gofmt -l .`
# flagged 446 of 460 .go files whose formatting was correct, and a tool-written LF line inside a
# CRLF vite.config.ts made vitest fail 121 of 121 files at registration — which was written off as
# a mysterious repo-directory curse for weeks.
#
# `text eol=lf` means: normalise to LF in the repository, and check out LF on every platform. The
# editors this project is developed in all handle LF on Windows; the gates do not handle CRLF.
# The default for everything not named below. Without it, a file this list forgot -- go.mod,
# go.sum, requirements.txt, .gitignore -- is still checked out CRLF on a Windows clone, because
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM config, so a fresh clone has it on even
# when nothing global or local sets it. The explicit lines after this one are kept because they
# document which gates break, and because `text=auto` guesses while they do not.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.go   text eol=lf
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.tsx  text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.jsx  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF endings even when checked out on Windows. A CRLF shebang makes the
# Linux container fail with the very unhelpful "exec /entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory".
*.sh text eol=lf

# Dockerfiles and the env template are read by Linux tooling for the same reason.
Dockerfile*     text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf

# Binary assets: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.mmdb  binary

# PowerShell scripts keep CRLF: they only ever run on Windows, Windows PowerShell's own tooling
# writes CRLF, and nothing in CI or in a container reads them.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
